    "evidence_text": "More importantly, when existing predictive models use input representations for entities and relations, we can easily replace those representations with Dolores representations and significantly improve the performance of existing models.\n\nIn fact, we incorporate Dolores embeddings to improve the performance of the model proposed by Das et al. (  ).\n\nMore specifically, Dolores embeddings are task specific combination of the context-dependent and context-independent representations learned by our learner.\n\nGiven a downstream model, Dolores learns a weighted linear combination of the components of $R_t$ to yield a single vector for use in the embedding layer of the downstream model.\n\nWhile it is obvious that our embeddings can be used as features for new predictive models, it is also very easy to incorporate our learned Dolores embeddings into existing predictive models on knowledge graphs.\n\nThe only requirement is that the model accepts as input, an embedding layer (for entities and relations).\n\nIf a model fulfills this requirement (which a large number of neural models on knowledge graphs do), we can just use Dolores embeddings as a drop-in replacement.\n\nIn our evaluation below, we show how to improve several state-of-the-art models on various tasks simply by incorporating Dolores as a drop-in replacement to the original embedding layer.",
